WebKit: the proxy of a web process that ended is not destroyed.

When the page of a process that ended (crashed, was terminated by the client, ran out of memory) is
loaded again in a new process, WebPageProxy::launchProcess releases the old WebProcessProxy. On this
port that destruction aborts the UI process: crashDueToCheckedPtrToDeadObject, a CheckedRef released
on an object that was already scribbled, from ~BackgroundProcessResponsivenessTimer's RunLoop timers
(the timers' callbacks hold a CheckedRef to their owner). Reproduced with LazyWeb+'s Stop or Reload on
a page whose process does not answer (issue #862): the UI process died right after launching the
replacement process, with or without waiting for the socket to close first, and with the process
responsive or not. The cause is not found. Keeping the dead proxy (one reference leaked each time a
process ends) avoids it: the proxy is a few kilobytes, and a process ends only by a crash, a limit
or the person's choice.

--- a/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/WebProcessProxy.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/WebProcessProxy.cpp
@@ -1462,6 +1462,14 @@
     // to be deleted before we can finish our work.
     Ref protectedThis { *this };
 
+    // LazyOS: the proxy of a process that ended is never destroyed. Destroying it (when the page is
+    // reloaded in a new process, which releases the old proxy) aborts the UI process in
+    // crashDueToCheckedPtrToDeadObject, from ~BackgroundProcessResponsivenessTimer's RunLoop timers;
+    // seen on this port after WKPageTerminate and Reload (LazyWeb+, issue #862). The cause is not
+    // found; a dead proxy is a few kilobytes and a process ends only by a crash, a memory limit or
+    // the person's choice, so it is kept (a reference leaked once per ending).
+    ref();
+
     liveProcessesLRU().remove(*this);
 
     auto pages = mainPages();
